cien Sunni Talal are preparing a meal  just like any other day in the village  of Katra but their lives changed forever  when their daughters were murdered  something is terribly amiss at home and  they feel that void in everything they  do they helped us with everything they  were good girls they were always around  they enjoyed helping out the family now  has around-the-clock police protection  because they've sought justice they've  made powerful enemies in the village the  men accused of the crime are from a  higher caste with considerable influence  locally they're now fears of revenge  attacks we have to protect the family  there have been threats against them the  police are here but for how long a year  six months how long will they protect  people like us before the girls were  raped and hanged only two photos existed  of the cousins their mothers say the  girls were intelligent and studious and  had high hopes of escaping poverty now  many more pictures of their lifeless  bodies have gone round the world somehow  the families find solace in the shocking  images the photos remind us of them many  people came here to see them when we  look at the photos together we cry and  it brings back memories  dig dig little bear the murders have  brought immense grief to the families  and many girls have even less freedom  than before because of the fear of  further violence now my daughter's have  to stay at home with me I can't take  them to work in the fields so I watch  them here see it takes us to where her  daughter was murdered three suspects are  in custody and two policemen have been  removed from the force the officers  allegedly protected the suspects because  they were members of the same  influential caste  the family says it's only because  residents blocked access to the bodies  at the mango tree until the news media  arrived that a real investigation  followed but to this day the family's  charges are being called into doubt  they've even been ordered to take  lie-detector tests the village elder  says the events here have nothing to do  with caste what people need is better  education he says perhaps even  University he believes locking women up  at home is wrong there are only men in  his circle ask women in the village and  you'll likely hear it's time for men to  change their attitude nobody wants to  listen to us women if we go to the  police they say who are you nobody  listens to us here if found guilty the  accused would face the death penalty but  no one here really believes that women's  lives are likely to change anytime soon
 

